@startuml 05-state-pattern
!include _style.iuml
title State pattern: one class per incident status

interface IncidentState {
  + status() : IncidentStatus
  + allowedNext() : Set<IncidentStatus>
  + canMoveTo(target) : boolean
}
abstract class AbstractIncidentState {
  - status : IncidentStatus
  - allowedNext : Set<IncidentStatus>
  # AbstractIncidentState(status, allowedNext...)
}
class IncidentStates <<registry>> {
  - STATES : EnumMap<IncidentStatus, IncidentState>
  + {static} of(status) : IncidentState
}
class IncidentStateMachine {
  + {static} canTransition(from, to) : boolean
  + {static} requireTransition(from, to)
}
class Incident {
  - status : IncidentStatus
  + acknowledge(now)
  + resolve(text, now)
  ...
}
class InvalidStateTransitionException

IncidentState <|.. AbstractIncidentState
AbstractIncidentState <|-- NewState
AbstractIncidentState <|-- ClassifiedState
AbstractIncidentState <|-- AssignedState
AbstractIncidentState <|-- AcknowledgedState
AbstractIncidentState <|-- InProgressState
AbstractIncidentState <|-- PendingInfoState
AbstractIncidentState <|-- ResolvedState
AbstractIncidentState <|-- VerifiedState
AbstractIncidentState <|-- ClosedState
AbstractIncidentState <|-- CancelledState
AbstractIncidentState <|-- DuplicateState

IncidentStates o--> "11" IncidentState
IncidentStateMachine ..> IncidentStates : looks up
Incident ..> IncidentStateMachine : every move goes through
IncidentStateMachine ..> InvalidStateTransitionException : throws

note bottom of NewState
  NewState() { super(NEW, CLASSIFIED, CANCELLED, DUPLICATE); }
  Adding a status = add one class; nothing else changes.
end note
@enduml
